-- Q3212: Derivatives and Hedging [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): A written (sold) call option profits when the stock price stays below the strike price, allowing the option to expire worthless so the writer keeps the premium received. At $40 current price with a $45 strike, the writer benefits if the stock remains below $45.
Wrong (A): A written call loses money when the stock price rises above the strike price. The writer must deliver shares at $45 regardless of how high the market price goes, creating losses.
Wrong (B): Writing call options on one''s own stock is permitted under U.S. GAAP. ASC 815 addresses the accounting treatment for such instruments.
Wrong (C): While a written call has theoretically unlimited loss potential if the stock rises, this describes risk, not the profit condition asked about.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3212;

-- Q3213: Derivatives and Hedging [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 815, a collar strategy involves buying a put option (establishing a price floor) and selling a call option (establishing a price ceiling) on the same underlying commodity. The premium received from selling the call partially or fully offsets the cost of buying the put, reducing net hedging cost while providing downside protection.
Wrong (A): Buying a call and selling a put at the same strike creates a synthetic long position (risk reversal), which increases price exposure rather than protecting against declines.
Wrong (C): Buying two calls at different strike prices creates a bull call spread, which profits from price increases rather than protecting against declines.
Wrong (D): Selling both a call and a put at the same strike creates a short straddle, which profits from low volatility but leaves the producer exposed to significant price declines.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3213;

-- Q3215: Derivatives and Hedging [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 815-30-35, the ineffective portion of a cash flow hedge gain or loss is recognized immediately in current period earnings. Only the effective portion is recorded in OCI and reclassified to earnings when the hedged transaction affects income. There is no materiality threshold for ineffectiveness.
Wrong (B): Deferring ineffectiveness in OCI is incorrect. OCI treatment applies only to the effective portion of cash flow hedges; ineffectiveness must flow through earnings immediately.
Wrong (C): There is no proportional allocation mechanism between OCI and earnings. The split is binary: effective portion to OCI, ineffective portion to earnings.
Wrong (D): ASC 815 does not provide a 10% or any other de minimis threshold for ignoring hedge ineffectiveness. All ineffectiveness must be recognized in earnings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3215;

-- Q3216: Derivatives and Hedging [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 815, when interest rates fall, the floating rate received on the swap decreases while the fixed rate paid remains constant. The company pays more than it receives, creating a net liability position. The swap has a negative fair value from the company''s perspective because the present value of future fixed payments exceeds floating receipts.
Wrong (B): This reverses the analysis. The company receives floating (which decreased) and pays fixed (unchanged), so it is worse off, not better off. A positive fair value would require rates to rise.
Wrong (C): Swaps do not always net to zero. While they start at approximately zero fair value, subsequent rate movements cause the swap to become an asset or liability.
Wrong (D): Hedge designation does not eliminate fair value changes. The swap still has a measurable fair value; hedge accounting only affects where gains and losses are reported.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3216;

